  "text_md": "## Table 3 Velocity as a function of time.\n\n|    t |   v(t) (m/s) |\n|------|--------------|\n|    0 |              |\n|   10 |       227.04 |\n|   15 |       362.78 |\n|   20 |       517.35 |\n| 22.5 |       602.97 |\n|   30 |       901.67 |\n\n- a) Determine the value of the velocity at t =16 seconds using quadratic splines.\n- b)   Using the quadratic splines as velocity functions, find the distance covered by the rocket from\n3. Using the quadratic splines as velocity functions; find the acceleration of the rocket at t =16s .\n\n## Solution\n\n- a Since there are six data points, five quadratic splines pass through them.\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nThe equations are found as follows.\n\n+bt + c passes through t =0 and\n\n1. Each quadratic spline passes through two consecutive data points.\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n+bzt + €2 passes through t =10 and t = 15 \\_\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n+b,t + c; passes through t =15 and t = 20\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\n+b4t + €4 passes through t = 20 and t = 22.5 .\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->",
